    "case_id": "391",
    "case_summary": "A man in his 40s presented with:- Gradual hair loss of the eyelashes, eyebrows, beard, and scalp for 4 years- Swelling and painful hand joints for 13 months- Multiple asymptomatic nodules on the upper arms for 40 daysNo fever, analgesia or hypesthesia reported. No previous infections, family history, or personal medical history of autoimmune diseases or psoriasis. Previous treatments for joint symptoms showed no improvements over 1 year.\n\n- Diffuse infiltration of the face with loss of skin creases and diminished facial hair- All joints of both hands were swollen- Multiple asymptomatic nodules without ulceration on upper arms- No decreased sensation to pain, temperature and/or touch- No neuropathic changes found\n\nLaboratory Tests:- Erythrocyte sedimentation rate: 27 mm/h (slightly elevated)- Rheumatoid factor: 70.1 IU/mL (significantly elevated)- Normal results for: C-reactive protein, anticyclic citrullinated peptide, antinuclear antibodies, and globulin levelsImaging Studies:- X-ray and joint ultrasound showed: osteoarthritis, synovitis, flexor tendon tenosynovitis, and bone erosions- Ultrasound revealed: enlarged axillary, cervical, and inguinal lymph nodes and enlarged spleen- Fundus fluorescein angiography showed uveitisPathology:Image Title: Skin biopsy from upper arm noduleImage Description: Diffuse infiltrate of foamy histiocytes in the dermis (hematoxylin-eosin stain)Additional Finding: Numerous acid-fast bacilli present (acid-fast stain)",
